# Read hooks

**Reads use hooks. Writes do not.** A hook manages state over time — fetch on
mount, refetch when inputs change. See `07-writes-and-handlers`.

## The pattern

```ts
export function usePetbarnAnimals(locationIds: string[]) {
  const [animals, setAnimals] = useState<AnimalEdge[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const idKey = locationIds.join(",");

  useEffect(() => {
    if (locationIds.length === 0) { setLoading(false); return; }

    let cancelled = false;
    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    fetchPetbarnAnimals(locationIds)
      .then(r => { if (!cancelled) setAnimals(r?.edges ?? []); })
      .catch(e => { if (!cancelled) setError(e.message); })
      .finally(() => { if (!cancelled) setLoading(false); });

    return () => { cancelled = true; };
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [idKey]);

  return { animals, loading, error };
}
```

## Rules

- **Three states, always** — data, loading, error. A fetch has three outcomes.
- **The cancelled flag is not optional.** Without it, a request resolving after
  unmount sets state on nothing, and a slow request for an old input can land
  after a fast one for the new input, silently overwriting correct data.
- **Never put an object or array in a dependency array.** React compares by
  identity; a literal built during render is new every time. The effect
  re-runs, sets state, re-renders, re-runs — an infinite loop hammering the
  org. Derive a stable primitive and depend on that.
- **The `exhaustive-deps` disable is the one sanctioned lint override**, and
  only for the stability workaround above. It needs a comment saying why.
- **Initial state mirrors the shape.** Arrays start `[]`, single objects start
  `null`. An array starting `null` forces every consumer to guard.
- **Guard the empty input case** and return early. Do not call the org with an
  empty bind.
- **Never set state during render.** Setters belong in effects and handlers.
  Setting during render is an instant infinite loop.

## Composing

Small single-purpose hooks; one composing hook per screen.

```ts
export function usePetbarnHome() {
  const loc = usePetbarnLocations();
  const animals = usePetbarnAnimals(loc.locations);
  const stats = usePetbarnStats(loc.locationIds);

  return {
    animals: animals.animals,
    loading: loc.loading || animals.loading,
    error: loc.error ?? animals.error,
    stats: stats.stats,
    statsLoading: stats.loading,
  };
}
```

- **Combine loading with `||`** for anything the page must wait for, or it
  flashes an empty state between sequential fetches.
- **First error wins** (`??`) — it is the root cause; later errors are symptoms.
- **Secondary data gets its own flag.** Stats must not gate the table. A failed
  stat card must not hide the animals.
